ticket_data.description:
`Value(None, output_field=CharField())` represents SQL `NULL` and compiles without bound parameters. This query worked on 6.0 on SQLite:

{{{#!py
from django.db import models

class Person(models.Model):
    metadata = models.JSONField()

def run():
    qs = Person.objects.filter(metadata__in=[models.Value(None, models.CharField())])
    print(qs)
}}}
{{{#!sql
SELECT "app_person"."id",
       "app_person"."metadata"
FROM "app_person"
WHERE "app_person"."metadata" IN (NULL)
LIMIT 21
}}}

But after 9b8e4c6d7d47f7b2e96817ba334e9fd53c10a0df (6.1), it crashes on SQLite with:
{{{#!py
  File "/Users/jwalls/django/django/db/models/lookups.py", line 349, in <genexpr>
    self.resolve_expression_parameter(compiler, connection, sql, param)
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
  File "/Users/jwalls/django/django/db/models/fields/json.py", line 444, in resolve_expression_parameter
    and params[0] not in connection.ops.jsonfield_datatype_values
        ~~~~~~^^^
IndexError: list index out of range
}}}
And differently on 6.1.2 after #37377, at the database level:
{{{#!py
sqlite3.ProgrammingError: Incorrect number of bindings supplied. The current statement uses 3, and there are 4 supplied.
}}}

